AB  - Discusses a range of policies concerning food pricing and distribution, public employment schemes, and social security. In many parts of Sub-Saharan Africa the term for being poor is synonymous with lacking kin or friends, and the dissolution of family and ethnic ties has weakened community support systems in many developing countries. Developing countries have adopted various forms of food subsidy such as food stamps and food supplementation schemes, which can be used to raise the real incomes of the direct beneficiaries and provide a safety net to protect a wider group of the poor against collapses in their real incomes. Rural public employment schemes maintain and create rural infrastructure such as road-building, irrigation, soil conservation, and afforestation programs, and can reduce poverty by providing employment to those in need. A promising approach for some countries involves transfers based on indicators of need that can be monitored through local health care and community-based systems.
