TY  - JOUR
TI  - O0111 HOME REHABILITATION OF SEVERE MALNUTRITION USING LOCALLY PRODUCED AND IMPORTED SOLID READY TO USE FOODS (RTUF) AFTER 1 WEEK INPATIENT CARE.
AU  - Elhadji Issakha Diop
AU  - N. I. Dossou
AU  - André Briend
AU  - M. A. Yaya
AU  - M. M. Ndour
AU  - Salimata Wade
JO  - Journal of Pediatric Gastroenterology and Nutrition
PY  - 2004
VL  - 39
IS  - Supplement 1
SP  - S50
EP  - S51
DO  - 10.1097/00005176-200406001-00113
AB  - Introduction: The rehabilitation phase of uncomplicated severe malnutrition can be achieved in the community by using a solid ready to use food (RTUF) instead of a liquid milk based diet (1). This approach shortens the duration of inpatient care, and is less disruptive for families and decreases the risk of nosocomial infections. RTUF has the same nutritional composition in relation to energy as the liquid milk based WHO F100 diet, is resistant to bacterial contamination and gives higher weight gains (2). Our study aimed to test the acceptability of locally made vs imported RTUFs and to assess their efficacy in promoting weight gain at home. Compared to the imported product, local RTUF does not contain lactoserum nor maltodextrines, but more sugar. Methods: Acceptability of local vs imported RTUF was first compared in a 2-day test at the beginning of the rehabilitation phase. 61 senegalese children received local or imported RTUFs alternately 3 times a day, each child being his own control. After one week, after they recovered their appetite, children were randomized to receive either local or imported RTUF for home treatment, until their weight for height Z score was > −1.5. Weight gain was then compared between the two groups. Results: Mean energy intakes from the 2 foods were not different: 3407 kJ (SD: 1430) and 3156 kJ (SD: 961) in the local and imported RTUFs, respectively (ns). After excluding patients who died (3), were transferred (3) or dropped out (8), 23 and 24 children treated at home using the local and imported RTUF respectively were available for analysis. Mean weight gain at home were 7.9 g kg-1 day-1 (SD: 4.8) and 8.1 g kg-1 day-1 (SD: 4.2) in the local and in the imported groups respectively. Duration of rehabilitation was not different between the two groups either: 35 days (SD: 21 ) and 33 days (SD: 16 ) respectively. Conclusion: These findings suggest that locally prepared RTUF was as well accepted as the imported version and leads to comparable weight gain during home rehabilitation. The technology for producing RTUF is not sophisticated, hence, RTUF can be produced locally in more developing countries to manage uncomplicated severe malnutrition.
